# Code Style and Architecture

## General principles
- Prefer simple, readable, maintainable solutions over clever abstractions.
- Keep functions and components focused on one responsibility.
- Avoid large files; split logic into reusable modules when needed.

## Next.js and React
- Use App Router patterns and Server Components by default.
- Use Client Components only when interactivity or browser APIs are required.
- Keep route-level UI in `app/` and shared UI in reusable component folders.
- Co-locate feature-specific code close to the route or feature that uses it.

## TypeScript conventions
- Avoid `any`; use explicit types, unions, or generics.
- Define domain types/interfaces for core entities (for example, Case and UserProfile).
- Type all function inputs and outputs for non-trivial logic.

## Data and state
- Keep business logic out of UI markup-heavy components.
- Centralize Supabase data access in dedicated helpers/services.
- Validate incoming data and handle loading/empty/error states explicitly.

## UI and styling
- Use Tailwind utility classes consistently; avoid ad-hoc inline styles.
- Build mobile-first layouts and verify key breakpoints.
- Keep visual distinction between TraaS and Tech Bootcamp consistent.

## Reliability and safety
- Do not remove existing behavior without explicit confirmation.
- Preserve mock data paths until real Supabase flow is verified.
- Add concise comments only where intent is not obvious from code.

## Delivery workflow
- Before major edits: state plan and touched files.
- Implement in small steps and verify each step before moving on.
- Run lint/type checks when changes are substantial.
